# Better Accessibility (Figma)

Accessibility problems are cheapest to fix on the canvas and most expensive to fix in production. This skill finds them in Figma files and repairs them in place, using design system variables rather than hardcoded values so the fix propagates instead of drifting.

## What this skill can and cannot check

Figma has no DOM, so there is nothing to inspect for ARIA roles, semantic HTML, or keyboard tab order. What exists on the canvas is colour, size, spacing, layer structure, component variants, and annotations. This skill audits **the design-time proxies** for accessibility and flags the rest as "verify in build."

| Checkable in Figma | Only verifiable in code |
| --- | --- |
| Contrast ratios (text, icons, borders, states) | Actual ARIA roles and names |
| Text size, line height, measure | Real screen-reader announcement order |
| Target size and spacing | Keyboard trap behaviour |
| Layer order as a *proxy* for reading order | Live focus management |
| Presence of focus/error/disabled variants | Motion-reduction media queries |
| Alt-text and heading-level annotations | Form validation announcements |
| Reflow behaviour via auto layout resizing | Zoom/reflow at real viewport sizes |

Never claim a design "passes WCAG." A design can only be *contrast-conformant and structurally sound*; conformance is a property of the built product.

## Prerequisites

- Remote Figma MCP server connected. Verify by calling `whoami` before anything else.
- A target file key or a node URL containing `node-id`. Ask for one rather than guessing.
- Full seat for write access. Dev seats are read-only outside drafts — in that case, produce the report and stop, don't attempt fixes.

If Figma MCP tools appear as deferred tools, batch-load the schemas in one call:
`ToolSearch query="select:use_figma,get_metadata,get_screenshot,search_design_system,whoami"`

Include `better-accessibility-figma` in the comma-separated `skillNames` parameter when calling `use_figma`.

## Workflow

Run these in order. Do not start fixing before the full audit is complete — early fixes change node IDs and invalidate the inventory.

### 1. Inventory

Call `get_metadata` on the target node to get the layer tree, then `get_screenshot` for the visual. The screenshot catches things the tree can't (a label that visually collides, an icon that vanishes into its background); the tree catches things the screenshot can't (a hidden layer still in reading order, a detached instance).

Identify which library each instance comes from. A violation inside a foundation token or a published component is a systemic bug affecting every consumer downstream; the same violation in a single screen is local. Say which kind you found — it changes who owns the fix and how urgent it is.

### 2. Audit passes

Run all six. Report findings even when you can't auto-fix them.

**Pass 1 — Contrast.** Compute the WCAG 2.2 ratio for every text fill against its resolved backdrop, walking up the parent chain past transparent fills. Thresholds: 4.5:1 normal text, 3:1 for text ≥24px or ≥18.66px bold, 3:1 for icons, focus indicators, input borders, and any non-text element carrying meaning. Check *every variant state*, not just default — disabled and placeholder styles are where most failures live, and "it's disabled so it doesn't count" is only true if the control is genuinely non-interactive. Gradients and images behind text: measure the worst-case region, not the average.

**Pass 2 — Typography.** Flag body text below 16px, line height below 1.5× for paragraph text, letter-spacing below -1%, and measure beyond ~75 characters. Flag any text style that carries meaning by weight or colour alone. Flag all-caps runs longer than a short label — they slow reading for dyslexic users and are announced letter-by-letter by some screen readers when the underlying string is also uppercase. Watch display and editorial typefaces used at small sizes: thin strokes and high-contrast cuts can measure as contrast-conformant while reading poorly below 16px, since the ratio is computed from the fill colour and ignores stroke weight.

**Pass 3 — Target size.** WCAG 2.2 (2.5.8) sets a 24×24px minimum for pointer targets, with a spacing exception; 44×44px is the practical mobile target. Measure the *hit area*, not the icon glyph — a 16px icon in a 24px frame with no padding is a fail. Flag adjacent targets closer than 24px apart.

**Pass 4 — Structure and order.** Layer order in Figma is bottom-to-top; reading order is top-to-bottom. Auto layout frames generally serialize in a predictable order, absolutely positioned children do not. Flag any absolutely positioned interactive element, any layer whose visual position contradicts its tree position, and any frame where the visual reading order and the layer order disagree. Flag generic layer names (`Frame 427`, `Rectangle 12`) on anything interactive — the layer name is what the developer inherits.

**Pass 5 — States and annotations.** Every interactive component should have default, hover, focus, active, disabled, and where relevant error states as real variants. A missing focus variant is a high-severity finding: if it isn't designed, it gets built as `outline: none`. Also check for alt-text annotations on meaningful images, heading-level annotations (H1–H6) on text that acts as a heading, and labels on every input — placeholder text is not a label.

**Pass 6 — Reflow and independence.** Check auto layout resizing: fixed-width text containers break at 200% zoom. Flag hug-vs-fill choices that will clip translated strings — French runs roughly 15–25% longer than English, which matters for anything shipping under RGAA. Verify no information is conveyed by colour alone (a red border with no error text, a green dot with no label), and that any status carries a second channel: icon, text, or pattern.

### 3. Report

Produce the report before touching anything. Use this structure:

```
## Accessibility audit — [file/frame name]

**Scope:** [nodes audited] · **Standard:** WCAG 2.2 AA
**Summary:** X blocking · Y should-fix · Z verify-in-build

### Blocking
- [Layer name → node-id] Finding. Measured: 3.07:1, needs 4.5:1. Fix: [specific change]

### Should fix
...

### Verify in build
- [Things Figma cannot confirm — keyboard order, announcements, motion]
```

Severity: **Blocking** = a WCAG A/AA failure a user will hit. **Should fix** = degrades experience or fails AAA. **Verify in build** = not determinable on canvas.

Order findings by user impact, not by how easy they are to fix.

### 4. Fix

Apply fixes with `use_figma`. Rules:

- **Bind to variables, never hardcode.** If the correct value is `Core/color/text-primary`, bind the variable; don't paste the hex. A hardcoded fix is a future drift bug.
- **If no suitable variable exists, say so and propose one** rather than inventing a hex. Use `search_design_system` to check before concluding it doesn't exist.
- **Fix at the highest level that resolves it.** If forty screens fail because one Core token is wrong, fix the token and note the blast radius. Do not patch forty instances.
- **Never detach an instance to fix it.** Detaching resolves the symptom and breaks the system. If the fix requires detaching, it's a component-level finding — report it instead.
- **Preserve intent.** If darkening a brand colour would break brand compliance, present the tradeoff and options (larger text, a darker text-only variant, added weight) rather than silently overriding the brand.
- Re-screenshot after fixing and re-run Pass 1 on changed nodes to confirm the fix landed.

### Plugin API gotchas

`use_figma` executes plugin-API JavaScript. Load fonts before any text mutation (`await figma.loadFontAsync(node.fontName)`) or writes throw. Write top-level `await` and `return` — don't wrap in an async IIFE, and don't call `figma.closePlugin()`. `figma.notify()` is not implemented. Return the affected node IDs so findings can be re-verified.

## Regulatory notes

WCAG 2.2 AA is the unifying technical standard across the three frameworks below, so audit against it once and map the result.

- **US (ADA / Section 508):** Section 508 references WCAG 2.0 AA; ADA Title II now references WCAG 2.1 AA. Auditing to 2.2 AA satisfies both.
- **UK (Equality Act / PSBAR):** public sector bodies target WCAG 2.2 AA and must publish an accessibility statement — flag findings that would need disclosing.
- **France (RGAA):** RGAA is a test methodology layered on WCAG, with its own criteria numbering and a mandatory declaration. Findings map to WCAG criteria; if the user needs RGAA criterion numbers, say that mapping is required rather than guessing at numbering.

When the user works bilingually, note that string-length expansion (Pass 6) is a compliance issue under RGAA, not just a layout nicety.


